WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.420
(quirky music)

00:00:01.420 --> 00:00:04.323
- [Announcer] It's part of the
secret sauce in your Big Mac,

00:00:04.323 --> 00:00:07.010
the condiment in your Reuben.

00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:09.587
It's creamy yet tangy, zesty,

00:00:09.587 --> 00:00:13.470
and named after a breathtaking
part of New York state.

00:00:13.470 --> 00:00:16.360
I'm talking about
Thousand Island dressing.

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:18.290
The origin of Thousand Island dressing

00:00:18.290 --> 00:00:21.650
traces back to the highest
ranks of American society.

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:23.241
How did it all come to be?

00:00:23.241 --> 00:00:26.163
Well, that's where things get interesting.

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:29.910
Two tales lie at the heart of

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:32.640
the Thousand Island dressing dispute.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:34.340
There is Allen Benas,

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:36.600
former owner of the Thousand Island Inn,

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:40.130
where he claims the dressing
was first served to the public.

00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:43.470
And then, there's George
Boldt, a legendary hotelier

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:44.940
whose chef created the dressing

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:47.240
and had it served at his hotels.

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:51.710
We tracked down a George Boldt
expert to explain his story.

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:54.210
- [Shane] George Boldt
was a Prussian immigrant.

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:56.330
He rose to one of the most prominent

00:00:56.330 --> 00:00:58.670
hotel men of the nation.

00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:00.480
- [Announcer] He also loved to vacation

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:01.683
in the Thousand Islands.

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:05.810
- [Shane] One day, Mr.
Boldt and his wife Louise

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:08.480
were taking a boat ride
on one of the yachts,

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:10.940
and the chef on board
forgot the salad dressing,

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:14.210
so he improvised and created
what became to be known as

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:15.600
Thousand Island dressing.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:17.830
He loved the dressing so
much, he begins serving it

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:20.080
at the Waldorf Astoria
Hotel in New York City.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.462
- [Announcer] But according
to the other guy, Allen Benas,

00:01:22.462 --> 00:01:24.510
it's nothing but hearsay.

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:26.277
- [Allen] The dressing was originated

00:01:26.277 --> 00:01:30.910
by the wife of a Clayton fishing
guide named Sophia LaLonde.

00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:34.240
She gave the recipe to the
original owners of the hotel,

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:36.590
where it was first served
to the dining public.

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:40.860
After we purchased the
hotel, and going through,

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:42.120
looking at everything,

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:45.190
in the safe was a sheet of
paper with a recipe on it,

00:01:45.190 --> 00:01:47.150
and I wasn't sure what it was.

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:51.217
I took it out to our kitchen
staff, and the head cook said,

00:01:51.217 --> 00:01:52.787
"It looks to me like a recipe for

00:01:52.787 --> 00:01:54.534
"Thousand Islands dressing."

00:01:54.534 --> 00:01:57.270
- [Announcer] So, will
we ever learn the truth?

00:01:57.270 --> 00:02:00.995
- [Allen] Nothing is more
popular than competing legends.

00:02:00.995 --> 00:02:01.828
- [Announcer] Mmm.

00:02:01.828 --> 00:02:03.026
Or as delicious.

00:02:03.026 --> 00:02:05.609
(quirky music)

